uggingface.co cite35†Hengli Li ,\nL65: \nL66: cite36†Image cite37†Kaiyan Zhang ,\nL67: \nL68: [Button: Che Jiang] ,\nL69: \nL70: cite38†Image cite39†Youbang Sun ,\nL71: [Button: Ermo Hua] ,\nL72: \nL73: cite40†Image cite41†Yuxin Zuo ,\nL74: \nL75: cite42†Image cite43†Xingtai Lv ,\nL76: \nL77: cite44†Image cite45†Qizheng Zhang ,\nL78: \nL79: cite46†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co cite47†Lin Chen ,\nL80: \nL81: [Button: Fanghao Shao] ,\nL82: \nL83: [Button: Bo Xue] ,\nL84: \nL85: [Button: Yunchong Song] ,\nL86: \nL87: cite48†Image cite49†Zhenjie Yang ,\nL88: \nL89: cite50†Image cite51†Ganqu Cui ,\nL90: \nL91: [Button: Ning Ding] ,\nL92: \nL93: cite52†Image cite53†Jianfeng Gao ,\nL94: \nL95: cite54†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co cite55†Xiaodong Liu ,\nL96: \nL97: [Button: Bowen Zhou] ,\nL98: \nL99: [Button: Hongyuan Mei]\nL100: \nL101: +1 authors\nL102: ## Abstract\nL103: \nL104: FlowRL enhances LLM reinforcement learning by matching the full reward distribution through flow balancing, improving diversity and performance over reward-maximizing methods.\nL105: \nL106: Generated by cite56†Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct L107: We propose cite57†FlowRL : matching the full cite58†reward distribution via cite59†flow balancing instead of maximizing rewards in large language model (LLM) reinforcement learning (RL). Recent advanced reasoning models adopt cite60†reward-maximizing methods (\\eg, cite61†PPO and cite62†GRPO ), which tend to over-optimize dominant reward signals while neglecting less frequent but valid reasoning paths, thus reducing diversity.\nL108: In contrast, we transform scalar rewards into a cite63†normalized target distribution using a cite64†learnable partition function , and then minimize the reverse KL divergence between the policy and the target distribution. We implement this idea as a flow-balanced optimization method that promotes cite65†diverse exploration and cite66†generalizable reasoning trajectories .\nL109: We conduct experiments on math and cite67†code reasoning tasks: cite57†FlowRL achieves a significant average improvement of 10.0% over cite62†GRPO and 5.1% over cite61†PPO on math benchmarks, and performs consistently better on cite67†code reasoning tasks. These results highlight reward distribution-matching as a key step toward efficient exploration and diverse reasoning in LLM cite68†reinforcement learning .\nL110: cite69†View arXiv page†arxiv.org cite70†View PDF†arxiv.org cite71†GitHub 182†github.com cite28†Add to collection L111: \nL112:   * cite72†Image†cdn-uploads.huggingface.co L113: ### Community\nL114: \nL115: cite31†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L116: \nL117: cite26†daixuancheng L118: \nL119: Paper author Paper submitter cite73†Sep 19, 2025 L120: \nL121: •\nL122: \nL123: cite73†edited Sep 19, 2025 L124:   * We propose FlowRL, a policy optimization algorithm that shifts from reward maximization to reward distribution matching via flow balance, encouraging diverse reasoning path exploration while addressing the inherent mode-collapse limitations of existing RL methods.\nL125:   * We introduce length normalization and importance sampling to enable effective training on variable-length CoT reasoning, addressing gradient explosion and sampling mismatch issues.\nL126:   * FlowRL outperforms GRPO and PPO by 10.0% and 5.1% respectively across math benchmarks and demonstrates strong generalization on code reasoning tasks, with diversity analysis confirming substantially more diverse solution exploration.\nL127: Reply\nL128: \nL129: cite74†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L130: \nL131: cite75†librarian-bot L132: \nL133: cite76†Sep 20, 2025 L134: \nL135: This is an automated message from the cite77†Librarian Bot . I found the following papers similar to this paper.\nL136: \nL137: The following papers were recommended by the Semantic Scholar API\nL138:   * cite78†Revisiting LLM Reasoning via Information Bottleneck (2025)\nL139:   * cite79†RL-PLUS: Countering Capability Boundary Collapse of LLMs in Reinforcement Learning with Hybrid-policy Optimization (2025)\nL140:   * cite80†Implicit Actor Critic Coupling via a Supervised Learning Framework for RLVR (2025)\nL141:   * cite81†COPO: Consistency-Aware Policy Optimization (2025)\nL142:   * cite82†Geometric-Mean Policy Optimization (2025)\nL143:   * cite83†Inpainting-Guided Policy Optimization for Diffusion Large Language Models (2025)\nL144:   * cite84†Posterior-GRPO: Rewarding Reasoning Processes in Code Generation (2025)\nL145: Please give a thumbs up to this comment if you found it helpful!\nL146: \nL147: If you want recommendations for any Paper on Hugging Face checkout cite85†this Space\nL148: \nL149: You can directly ask Librarian Bot for paper recommendations by tagging it in a comment: `@librarian-bot recommend`\nL150: \nL151: Reply\nL152: \nL153: cite86†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L154: \nL155: cite87†WpythonW L156: \nL157: cite88†Sep 20, 2025 L158: ## What to feed into MLP for Z_φ(x) in decoder-only architecture?\nL159: \nL160: The partition function `Z_φ(x)` is implemented as a 3-layer MLP taking prompt representation `x` as input. What exactly should be fed into the MLP for decoder-only models?\nL161: ### Options:\nL162: \nL163:   1. Last prompt token - hidden state of the final token before generation starts\nL164:   2. Prompt pooling - mean/max pooling over all prompt token hidden states\nL165:   3. Separator token - add special token between prompt and response\nL166: \nL167: Which approach is most common for this use case?\nL168: \nL169:   *   *   * [Button: 3 replies]\nL170: ·\nL171: \nL172: cite29†Image L173: \nL174: cite30†xuekai L175: \nL176: Paper author cite89†Sep 21, 2025 L177: \nL178: Sorry for the confused part about Log_Z! I'll detail this for you and update our paper ASAP.\nL179: From the flow perspective: Log_Z measures the probability flow from initial state S_0. Intuitively, it estimates a denominator - the sum of rewards across all possible paths, so we can convert to a distribution via reward/Z.\nL180: \nL181: From the implementation perspective: Since it's the initial state, we use the prompt encoded by the LM's last layer hidden states. To convert variable-length prompts to a scalar, we empirically take the mean. There are definitely other approaches here that we haven't explored yet\nL182: \nL183: 👍\nL184: 6\nL185: \nL186: 6\nL187: \nL188: +\nL189: \nL190: Expand 2 replies\nL191: \nL192: [Input: Reply in thread]\nL193: \nL194: cite90†Image L195: \nL196: cite91†grantsing L197: \nL198: cite92†Oct 16, 2025 L199: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper page - ScaleCUA: Scaling Open-Source Computer Use Agents with Cross-Platform Data (https://huggingface.co/papers/2509.15221)\nciteturn3369view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn3365view2\",\"id\":34}); Total lines: 237\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper page - Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration (https://huggingface.co/papers/2509.14760)\nciteturn3369view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn3365view2\",\"id\":38}); Total lines: 175\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper page - AToken: A Unified Tokenizer for Vision (https://huggingface.co/papers/2509.14476)\nciteturn3369view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn3365view2\",\"id\":41}); Total lines: 207\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper page - Evolving Language Models without Labels: Majority Drives Selection, No